{"folioDeck": 1, "exportedAt": "2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z", "meta": {"id": "faabc", "title": "Persian Alphabet", "subtitle": "39 letters and signs · both directions, as two cards per letter", "desc": "The Persian alphabet: 39 notes, in the alphabet's order — the thirty-two letters (the twenty-eight of Arabic and the four Persian added, پ چ ژ گ), then آ, the three short-vowel marks, the doubling mark, the hamza and the -an ending of لطفاً. The first deck of the Persian collection. Both directions: see a letter and recall its sound, or see a sound and recall the letter, each a card with its own schedule. Persian is written right to left, and most letters join the letters on both sides, changing shape as they do, so each letter is shown in its four shapes — alone, and at the start, middle and end of a word; the seven that never join the letter after them (ا د ذ ر ز ژ و) say so. Every note gives the romanisation the level decks use, the sound described in English words, and one everyday word that shows it, with a speaker beside the letter and the word. Persian spells some sounds with more than one letter — s with س ث ص, z with ز ذ ض ظ, t with ت ط, h with ه ح, the gargled g with غ ق — keeping the extra letters in words that came from Arabic; their cards say which is the everyday one, and the sound side names the word it asks for, so every card has one answer. The short vowels are usually NOT written, which is why the level decks romanise every word. The sounds are those of modern Iranian (Tehran) Persian, approximated in English as every learner's alphabet does. Sources: the standard description of modern Iranian Persian; the example words' romanisations are English Wiktionary's, via the kaikki.org extraction (CC BY-SA 4.0).", "author": "", "language": "en", "color": "#008763", "tags": ["persian", "farsi", "alphabet"], "glossMode": "site", "types": {"fa-abc": {"id": "fa-abc", "name": "Persian letter", "speechLang": "fa-IR", "fields": ["Letter", "Name", "Shapes", "Sound", 